== Qross CHANGES ==

Unreleased
* Port to Qt 6 and C++26: Qt 6.8 or later, CMake 3.21 and GCC 16. The
  library, the plugins and the console carry a -qt6 suffix, and the new
  pkg-config file qrosscore declares its Qt requirements. Qt 4 and Qt 5
  are no longer supported.
* Everything that depended on KDE is gone: the kdebindings tree vendored
  under src/bindings with its Smoke bindings and the Falcon and Java
  backends, the forms and translation modules, the action collection
  model and view, the GUI demo scripts, and the KJS, Lua, Java and
  Falcon interpreter entries.
* Core: Action and Manager no longer derive from QScriptable.
* API: Action is now Script, built once from a Config with a File or
  Code source, the interpreter (deduced from the extension when
  empty), option overrides and the published objects; nothing but
  the objects changes afterwards. run() replaces trigger() and
  returns std::expected; call() and evaluate() return the value or
  an Error of message, trace and line, and error() keeps the latest.
  Script is a QObject, not a QAction: the enabled state, text, icon,
  version, description and the XML round trip are gone with
  ActionCollection. The backend base class Script is now Context,
  created by Interpreter::createContext(), and Object::callMethod()
  returns std::expected. ErrorInterface and ChildrenInterface are
  gone; published objects are held through QPointer and
  AutoConnectSignals works for Manager objects too.
* Manager: hasInterpreter(), interpreterNameForFile(), addObject(),
  removeObject() and publishedObjects(); module(), deleteModules(),
  action(), hasAction(), actionCollection(), executeScriptFile(),
  strictTypesEnabled(), interpreterInfos(), addQObject(), qobject(),
  qobjectNames() and the started/finished signals are gone.
  InterpreterInfo declares its options as a QVariantMap of defaults.
* Scripts see self with name(), file(), directory(), interpreter()
  and functionNames(); callFunction() and evaluate() on self are
  gone.
* The core depends on QtCore only; qrosscore.pc no longer requires
  QtGui and QtXml. The console runs on QCoreApplication.
* Core: MetaFunction, the signal relay, connects through
  QMetaObject::connect and receives invocations in qt_metacall instead
  of building a metaobject per connection, so the core needs no private
  Qt headers. Backends get the arguments of a signal as QVariants.
* Core: interpreter plugins are looked up in QROSS_PLUGIN_PATH, the
  installation's library directory and Qt's library paths. Former
  interpreter names remain valid as aliases; qtscript names javascript.
* JavaScript: the QtScript backend is replaced by one on QQmlEngine. The
  interpreter is called javascript and claims *.js and *.es. Scripts see
  the script as self, the manager as Qross and every published object
  with its enum keys as properties; signals have connect(). Object
  literals convert to QRect, QPoint and QSize, and the qml option, on by
  default, imports QtQml so that QModelIndex and its relatives are usable.
  Script objects travel to C++ as Qross::Object::Ptr. Every run starts
  from a fresh engine. The widget constructors of the QtScript plugin
  are gone.
* Python: rewritten on the C API for Python 3.12 or later, without PyCXX
  and PyQt. Published objects are globals of a fresh __main__ module per
  run, which replaces the import hook; signals have connect() and
  disconnect(); script objects travel to C++ as Qross::Object::Ptr and
  come back as the same object; lists of QObject subclasses convert
  through the metatype system. The interpreter starts on first use,
  releases the GIL between calls so that signals reach callables from
  any thread, and finalizes with the Manager. The directory of the
  script is appended to sys.path.
* Ruby: rewritten on the C API for Ruby 3.2 or later. Ruby starts the way
  the ruby binary does, with RubyGems and the load path available.
  Scripts run with the script as self in a binding that keeps local
  variables across evaluate(); each QMetaObject gets a Ruby class under
  Qross:: with slots, signals, properties and enum keys as methods;
  connect() and disconnect() take a signal name or signature and a block
  or callable. Ruby runs only on the thread that started it, and the
  signal handlers it would install stay with the application. The
  safelevel option is gone with $SAFE, as is the QtRuby bridge.
* Tests: a CTest suite with QtTest units for the core and each backend
  plus the unittest scripts run through qrosstest, which now fails on
  script errors. BUILD_TESTING, on by default, replaces KDE4_BUILD_TESTS.
* Sources follow the style in .clang-format and use #pragma once.

0.3.2
* Qt 5 support alongside Qt 4; C++11; automoc.
* QtScript: values added to the engine get Auto ownership by default.
* Python: Qt import hook check and PythonInterpreter::setImportException.
* Fix a spurious crash.

0.3.1
* Python: QVariantList and QVariantMap can be passed to scripts.
* Python 2.7 is required.

0.3.0
* Python: capsules replace CObjects; QString casting fixes.

0.2.2
* Respect LIB_SUFFIX and libdir when installing.

0.2.1
* Do not use QFileIconProvider, which is not thread-safe.
* First tagged release of the fork: Kross from kdelibs 4 as Qross, with
  the KDE dependencies and the KJS backend removed, Ruby 1.8, and Python
  with PyQt4 conversions.

2.0 (Kross)
* The whole backend got replaced and is build up on Qt's metatypes now.
  This means we are able to throw any QObject/QVariant in and just use
  the slots as script-functions and the properties as script-members.
  So, we don't need wrappers/bindings any longer (and provided
  scripting-interfaces don't need to depend on Qross) on the one hand
  and and earn a more cleaner interface on the other. Beside the try
  to follow KISS, we should earn much more speed that way cause
  everything talks now Qt internal :)
* Port to Qt4 and KDE4
* Improved Python and Ruby backends a lot
* Added KDE JavaScript support using KjsEmbed
* Moved core from koffice/libs to kdelibs
* Moved backends to kdebindings

1.6
* Added qrossdebug and qrosswarning.
* Integrated ProxyFunction's.
* Python: Added unittest.
* Removed Argument class.
* Refactor of the tree-handling to improve the performance.
* Tweaks on Object and Value to improve the performance.

1.5.2
* API: Fix possible crash in Manager::getInterpreternameForFile()

1.5.1
* Python: Fix compile with gcc 3.3.6

1.5
* Ruby: Fix 124649: libruby-devel lost when build.
* Ruby: Fix crash cause of invalid rNode.
